# Set 3 · Q. No. 8(c) · Short Note: The National Commission on the Status of Women
@py pr_question 3 Q8c

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map for the short note: every branch is one headed point.
@note A 10-mark note: explain why Pakistan created a women's commission, its origins and legal status, what it does and has achieved, its weaknesses, and how to strengthen it.
@ask Short note: the NATIONAL COMMISSION on the STATUS of WOMEN (NCSW)
(1) Origins: Beijing's call for national machinery
(2) Establishment: 2000 ordinance; NCSW Act 2012
(3) Functions: review, monitor, recommend, research
(4) Achievements: the 2006 Act and later laws
(5) Weaknesses: powers, funds, devolution
(6) Strengthening the Commission
:::

## Decoding the question
A **10-mark note** (about **550–650 words**). The NCSW appears often in MCQs (it was set up under **Pervez Musharraf** — MCQ 2016) but has **never been a descriptive question**. Cover its **origins**, **legal status**, **functions**, **achievements** and **weaknesses**, ending with reforms.

## The model answer

### 1. Origins

#### i) The global call
The **Beijing Platform for Action** (==1995==), in **Critical Area H**, called on governments to create strong **national machinery** for the advancement of women. **CEDAW**, to which Pakistan acceded in ==1996==, requires states to review laws and policies for discrimination.

#### ii) Pakistan's earlier machinery
Pakistan already had a **Women's Division** (==1979==), later the Ministry of Women Development; the **Zari Sarfaraz Commission** (==1983–85==) had reported on women's status, but its report was shelved. A permanent, expert watchdog was missing.

### 2. Establishment and legal status

#### i) The 2000 ordinance
The **National Commission on the Status of Women** was set up by ordinance in ==2000==, under **Pervez Musharraf**.

#### ii) The NCSW Act 2012
The **NCSW Act 2012** gave the Commission a statutory basis and greater **financial and administrative autonomy**, strengthening its independence from the government of the day.

#### iii) Provincial commissions
After the **18th Amendment** (==2010==) made women's development largely provincial, provinces created their own bodies — the **Punjab Commission on the Status of Women** (==2014==), for example.

::: fig timeline | The NCSW and Pakistan's women's machinery.
@layout vertical
1979: Women's Division created
1983–85: Zari Sarfaraz Commission (report shelved)
1995: Beijing — call for national machinery (Area H)
1996: Pakistan accedes to CEDAW
2000: NCSW established by ordinance
2010: 18th Amendment devolves women's development
2012: NCSW Act — statutory autonomy
2014: Punjab Commission on the Status of Women
:::

### 3. Functions

#### i) Review laws and policies
The Commission examines laws, rules and government policies for their effect on women and recommends repeal or reform of discriminatory provisions.

#### ii) Monitor implementation
It monitors how laws and commitments — including CEDAW and the SDGs — are carried out, and how institutions treat women.

#### iii) Recommend and research
It commissions research, collects data and advises the government and parliament on women's rights.

#### iv) Link state and society
It works with women's organisations, legislators and international bodies, bringing civil-society evidence into the state.

### 4. Achievements

#### i) The Hudood reform
After years of campaigning by women's groups, a report of the NCSW contributed to the **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006**, which moved rape out of the Zina Ordinance and back into the Penal Code.

#### ii) Later laws
It supported the laws of ==2010–2016== — on **workplace harassment** (==2010==), **acid** and **anti-women practices** (==2011==), and **honour killing** (==2016==) — alongside the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==).

#### iii) An ally inside the state
With provincial commissions and harassment ombudspersons, the NCSW became an **ally of the women's movement inside the state** — part of what scholars call "state feminism" in Pakistan's reform phase.

### 5. Weaknesses

#### i) Advisory, not binding
Its recommendations are **not binding**; governments can delay or ignore them, so its influence depends on political will and on allies in parliament and civil society.

#### ii) Funds and staff
It has often been **under-funded** and short of staff, which limits its research, monitoring and reach beyond Islamabad.

#### iii) Devolution and overlap
After the 18th Amendment, the division of work between the federal Commission, provincial commissions and the federal **Ministry of Human Rights** (==2015==) has been unclear.

#### iv) Low public profile
Few women — especially in rural areas — know the Commission exists or how it can help them.

### 6. Strengthening the Commission

#### i) Powers
Require governments to **respond formally** to its recommendations within a fixed time and to table them in parliament.

#### ii) Resources and independence
Guarantee adequate **public funding**, transparent appointment of members and timely filling of vacancies.

#### iii) Coordination
Create a clear division of roles with provincial commissions and the Ministry of Human Rights, and a joint **gender data** system.

#### iv) Outreach
Open district-level complaint and information desks and use digital channels to reach women directly.

#### v) The line to take
The NCSW shows Pakistan has built the **machinery** for women's rights; what it still lacks is the **power and resources** to make that machinery decisive.

## Why this answer scores
- Places the NCSW in its **global context** (Beijing Area H, CEDAW) and Pakistan's earlier machinery.
- Gives its **legal history** precisely (ordinance ==2000==; **NCSW Act 2012**; provincial commissions).
- Balances **achievements** (2006 Act, later laws) against **weaknesses**, ending with reforms.
- A **timeline** figure shows the evolution of Pakistan's women's machinery.
